  "narrative": "I assessed every new discovery estimate on the signed scale already supplied, without reapplying direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the raw exposed-minus-comparator difference is more negative, i.e. stronger CRISPR dependency in exposed cells. For direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means less negative/weaker dependency in exposed cells.\n\nThe robustness checks for the three largest accepted associations are essentially confirmatory. H14 (R14) gives SMARCA4 loss vs SMARCA4 wild type within the Avana library a signed effect of +0.781 [0.760, 0.802], very close to the full-population H5 estimate and far from zero; cell counts are adequate. I accept it as a robust Avana-specific dataset association. H15 (R15) for MSI-high vs non-MSI WRN dependency in Avana is +0.794 [0.764, 0.825], again extremely large and precise; I accept it. H16 (R16) for TP53 loss USP7 dependency in Avana is +0.405 [0.330, 0.479], concordant with discovery R10 and validated R13; I accept it as robust in the major library. H23 (R17), the reversed KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2 association in Avana, is +0.796 [0.774, 0.818], nearly identical to the full-population reversed estimate; I accept it as strong evidence that the reversed direction is not driven by library composition. H25 (R18) shows MSI-high WRN dependency within adenocarcinoma at +0.784 [0.748, 0.820], and H29 (R21) shows KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 within adenocarcinoma at +0.793 [0.769, 0.817]. These are subgroup-specific consistency checks, not effect-modification tests, and both support persistence in the dominant lineage subtype.\n\nH28 (R20), the USP7/TP53 interaction by mutation burden >=5, is inconclusive: signed estimate +0.133 [-0.010, 0.275]. The interval crosses zero and, relative to the USP7 dependency scale, spans roughly small negative to very large positive relative change. It does not support a meaningful effect modification, but it does not rule one out. I keep it unresolved and defer it rather than chase a null/ambiguous interaction. H27 (R19) is invalid because the subgroup interaction design has an empty comparison cell (no TP53-loss MSI-high? Actually no exposed complement for the chosen mutation-burden cut; MSI-high appears fully contained in the high-burden stratum). It is not evaluable as specified; I close this investigation and register an alternative high-burden mean-difference check instead.\n\nThe POLR2A negative controls provide partial specificity evidence but are not uniformly definitive. H30 (R22) is a clean negative: SMARCA4 loss has signed +0.005 [-0.014, 0.024] on POLR2A. Even the upper bound is about 9% of the POLR2A mean scale, below the 10% practical threshold, so I reject the pre-specified meaningful positive control association and close it. H31 (R23) for MSI-high POLR2A is small in point estimate but imprecise: +0.0035 [-0.028, 0.035]; the interval includes zero and can reach beyond 10% relative magnitude, so I keep it unresolved/deferred. H32 (R24) for TP53 loss POLR2A is signed negative: -0.014 [-0.034, 0.006]. It rejects the original positive direction because the upper positive bound is far below practical significance, but the lower bound suggests a possible opposite small-to-moderate association. Rather than force the original arbitrary control direction to accommodate this, I reject H32 for its original direction and register a reversed POLR2A/TP53 proposal. H33 (R25) for KEAP1 loss POLR2A is +0.007 [-0.013, 0.027], small but with an upper bound just over the 10% relative scale; unresolved/deferred. Overall, the accepted dependency-specific effects are one to two orders of magnitude larger than these control estimates, so the controls make pure global technical confounding less likely, but two controls remain too imprecise to claim exact nulls.\n\nMultiplicity matters. The new wave contains many related checks, but the accepted large effects have intervals so far from zero that family-wise or Bonferroni-style 