ation with dependency_ERBB2 (H4/R4), and absence of MYC-amplification effects on dependency_DHX9 and dependency_RPL11 (H11/R11, H12/R12). POLR2A negative controls generally make a global technical confound less likely for the large accepted effects, although not every control is perfectly precise: SMARCA4-loss POLR2A is a clean negative (H30/R22), TP53-loss POLR2A rejects the original positive direction but may leave a small opposite effect (H32/R24, refinement H38), and MSI-high and KEAP1-loss POLR2A controls remain too imprecise to claim exact nulls (H31/R23, H33/R25). Residual uncertainty concerns effect modification and robustness: non-Avana checks for USP7/TP53 and NFE2L2/KEAP1 are pending (H36, H37), subtype and mutation-burden interactions remain mostly unresolved, and several smaller pathway questions remain underpowered or imprecise, including KRAS mutation on KRAS dependency, EGFR amplification on EGFR dependency, and TP53 loss on MDM2 dependency. The most informative next analysis is to test whether the validated KEAP1-loss reversed effect on NFE2L2 differs by adenocarcinoma subtype rather than merely persisting within it, while also prioritizing pending non-Avana robustness checks, the reversed POLR2A/TP53 control, and biologically plausible follow-ups such as STK11-loss modification of KRAS dependency, WRN/MSI within high mutation burden, and broad WRN/MYC replication-stress hypotheses. The newly registered NFE2L2/KEAP1 subtype interaction complements the already accepted adenocarcinoma mean difference and will better separate persistence from effect modification.",
